  "result": "DONE: Current backends have limited retry logic - only rate limits are retried; network errors cause immediate failure and rely on slower task-level retry.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n1. **Gemini backend has partial retry** (gemini.py:151-177): 3 attempts with 10/20/30s waits, but ONLY for HTTP 429 (rate limit). Network errors throw immediately.\n\n2. **Claude CLI has no network retry**: Session errors retry (line 251-262), but connection failures don't. Relies on task-level recovery.\n\n3. **Task-level retry exists but is expensive** (studio.py:775-780): Network errors classified for retry, but this restarts entire task - wasting context/tokens.\n\n4. **Best practice: Request-level exponential backoff**: Industry standard is 2s\u21924s\u21928s retries at HTTP layer, distinguishing retryable errors (timeout, connection reset) from terminal ones (auth, 400s).\n\n5. **Missing: Circuit breaker**: Extended outages should pause dispatching rather than burning retries.\n\n**Alternatives:**\n\n| Option | Effort | Impact |\n|--------|--------|--------|\n| Add retry to urllib calls | Low | Handles brief blips |\n| Circuit breaker in studio.py | Medium | Prevents retry storms |\n| Connection pooling | Medium | Reduces reconnection overhead |\n\n**Recommendation:** Add exponential backoff retry wrapper to `gemini.py` and `ollama.py` for `URLError`, `ConnectionResetError`, `socket.timeout`. Claude CLI subprocess is harder - may need to detect stderr patterns.\n\n**Sources:**\n- [Claude API Reliability: Error Handling Best Practices](https://www.developersdigest.tech/blog/claude-api-reliability-error-handling) \u2014 comprehensive retry patterns\n- [Best Practice Implementing Retry Logic in HTTP API Clients](https://medium.com/@API4AI/best-practice-implementing-retry-logic-in-http-api-clients-0b5469c08ced) \u2014 exponential backoff implementation",
